<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muslim Brotherhood</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">founded <a href="/years/1928">1928</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Society of the Muslim Brothers, al-Ikhwān al-Muslimūn</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Muslim Brotherhood is a transnational <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> Islamist organization founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1928 by the scholar and schoolteacher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hassan-al-banna">Hassan al-Banna</a>. It began as a religious and social movement that preached, taught the illiterate and set up hospitals, then entered politics against British colonial rule; its stated aim is a state ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">Sharia</a> under a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>. Members assassinated the prime minister Mahmoud El Nokrashy Pasha in 1948, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayyid-qutb">Sayyid Qutb</a> promoted takfirism in Milestones (1964); the group abandoned violence in the 1970s. Its candidate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohamed-morsi">Mohamed Morsi</a> became Egypt's first democratically elected president in 2012, but was overthrown in 2013, after which the group was banned in Egypt.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hassan-al-banna">Hassan al-Banna</a> founded the Society in the city of Ismailia in March 1928 with six workers of the Suez Canal Company. They appointed him their leader and vowed to work for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a>, taking the pledge that its members would be soldiers in the call to Islam. The Brothers regard their movement as the practical extension of the pan-Islamist movement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamal-al-din-al-afghani">Jamal al-Din al-Afghani</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-abduh">Muhammad Abduh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashid-rida">Rashid Rida</a>, and al-Banna was seen as its builder. He described the movement as a Salafiyya message, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> way, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> truth, a political organization, an athletic group, a cultural-educational union, an economic company and a social idea.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the Brotherhood established a Palestinian branch in October 1945, and a small branch appeared in Jordan around the turn of 1946. Governments in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> have cracked down on it repeatedly since 1948. Al-Banna led it until 1949, followed by Hassan al-Hudaybi until 1972 and Umar al-Tilmisani until 1986. The group remained on the fringe of Arab politics until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/six-day-war">Six-Day War</a> of 1967, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamism">Islamism</a> replaced secular Arab nationalism as a popular current after the Arab defeat. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hosni-mubarak">Hosni Mubarak</a> it was the largest opposition group in Egypt and had built a wide network of support through Islamic charities working among poor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a>.</p><p>The movement spread beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. It reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> in the last years of French rule, where Ahmad Sahnoun led it in 1953–1954 and its members took part in the uprising of 1954–1962, but it was marginalised under the one-party rule of the FLN after independence in 1962. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, the first members arrived from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> in the late 1950s and early 1960s, among them leaders who had fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Gamal Abdel Nasser</a>'s rule. Student groups grew into organisations, and mosques were run as community centres.</p><p>After the fall of Mubarak in 2011 the Egyptian Brotherhood was legalised. On 30 April 2011 it launched the Freedom and Justice Party, which won 235 of the 498 seats in that year's parliamentary elections, and in 2012 its candidate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohamed-morsi">Mohamed Morsi</a> won the presidency. Morsi was overthrown in 2013 and the group was banned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Brotherhood's best-known slogan is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> is the Solution. Its English-language website lists among its principles the Islamic Sharia as the basis for controlling the affairs of state and society and the unity of Islamic countries. Views of the group differ. Authors in Foreign Affairs wrote that it has at times used or supported violence but, since the 1970s, has disavowed it and sought to take part in Egyptian politics, while others question the sincerity of its statements. Many Islamist movements and parties have drawn on al-Banna's teachings.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Muslim Brotherhood” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The revolution of 2011 produced Egypt's first democratic election, won on 24 June 2012 by Mohamed Morsi of the <b>Muslim Brotherhood</b>, whose constitution, approved by 64 percent on a 33 percent turnout, split the country.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-egypt">Republic of Egypt · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Muslim Brotherhood</b>'s uprising of 1976–1982 ended in the Hama massacre of 1982, the single deadliest act of violence by any state on its own people in modern Arab history.</blockquote><a href="/empire/syrian-arab-republic">Syrian Arab Republic · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>From the 2010s it pursued an assertive foreign policy, opposing the <b>Muslim Brotherhood</b>, backing allied forces in Libya and Yemen, joining the boycott of Qatar from 2017 to 2021 and signing the Abraham Accords with Israel in 2020.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-arab-emirates">United Arab Emirates · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Islamists were present but did not lead: the <b>Muslim Brotherhood</b>’s old guard joined only when pushed by its young members, and Egypt’s mufti, Ali Gomaa, declared rising against Mubarak haram.</blockquote><a href="/movement/arab-spring">Arab Spring · People</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1928 Hassan al-Banna founded the <b>Muslim Brotherhood</b> in Ismailiyah, Egypt, arguably the first, largest and most influential modern Islamic political and religious organisation.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/islamism">Islamism · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
